1
00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:04,560
We're talking about the fact that carcinogenic agents

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:08,500
damage DNA, and when they damage DNA,

3
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:12,480
there are some key regulatory systems that can be damaged.

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,400
One key regulatory system that can be damaged

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,420
are proto-oncogenes, and when they're mutated,

6
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:20,040
they become oncogenes, and they can then result

7
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:21,260
in the formation of a tumor.

8
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:24,580
Another key regulatory system that can be damaged

9
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:26,240
are tumor suppressor genes.

10
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:28,360
And what do tumor suppressor genes do?

11
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:30,860
They regulate cell growth.

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,400
And so this is the discussion that we now want to have,

13
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,400
and that is what are tumor suppressor genes

14
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,960
and how could they result in tumor formation.

15
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:44,700
So the key idea here is that tumor suppressor genes,

16
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:48,160
they decrease, i.e. suppress, the risk of tumor formation.

17
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:52,840
And the two classic examples are p53 and retinoblastoma.

18
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,440
So let's take a moment to discuss each of these.

19
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:57,280
Now what does p53 do?

20
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:58,960
It regulates the cell cycle.

21
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:02,700
In particular, it regulates the G1 to S phase.

22
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:06,300
And the way I think about p53 is sort of like a traffic cop.

23
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:08,860
Let's imagine, let's take a whiteboard,

24
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:10,980
let's imagine that the cell would like to go

25
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:12,940
from the G1 to the S phase.

26
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:16,080
So before it can go from G1 to S phase,

27
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:18,180
it's got to sort of go past p53.

28
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:22,120
All right, now, when it walks past p53,

29
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:24,360
p53 is like a traffic cop,

30
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:27,140
and it basically says, all right, show me your DNA.

31
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:30,900
And when the cell shows p53 its DNA,

32
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:32,440
there's two possibilities.

33
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:35,520
If p53 looks at the DNA of the cell

34
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,740
and sees that the DNA is mutated,

35
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:40,980
then p53 will actually blow a molecular whistle,

36
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:44,200
and it'll call in DNA repair enzymes

37
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,600
and repair the DNA mutations

38
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,620
so that before the cell goes into S phase,

39
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:51,240
the DNA is repaired.

40
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:52,700
That's one possibility.

41
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:56,400
Another possibility is that p53 looks at the DNA of the cell

42
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:01,380
and says, there is no way this cell is going to go into S phase

43
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:04,180
because this cell's got way too many mutations

44
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:05,920
and we can't repair these mutations.

45
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,500
So in that case, what would p53 do?

46
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:10,480
It'll blow another molecular whistle,

47
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:13,200
and it'll call in a molecule called Bax.

48
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:15,220
And guess what Bax does?

49
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:19,100
Bax goes in, and it destroys BCL2.

50
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:23,060
So Bax goes in, and it destroys BCL2.

51
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:24,880
And you know what BCL2 is?

52
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:29,780
BCL2 is the molecule that stabilizes the mitochondrial membrane

53
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:32,880
so that cytochrome C doesn't leak out.

54
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:35,020
So if you knock out BCL2,

55
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:37,800
cytochrome C will actually leak out of the mitochondria,

56
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:40,160
and when cytochrome C leaks out of the mitochondria,

57
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:41,780
it will activate the caspases,

58
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:46,340
which will then create apoptosis of the cell.

59
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:51,860
And so p53 has the ability to induce apoptosis

60
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:55,520
when there's severe DNA mutation in a cell.

61
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:58,920
And again, the idea is you want that cell to undergo apoptosis

62
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,820
because it's got so many DNA mutations

63
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,320
that there's a risk that it could produce at cancer.

64
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:06,120
All right, so let's read together.

65
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:07,000
What do I say here?

66
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:09,460
P53 regulates the cell cycle,

67
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,660
in particular progression from the G1 to S phase.

68
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:15,980
Now again, what does p53 do?

69
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:18,240
In response to DNA damage,

70
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:22,100
p53 upregulates DNA repair enzymes.

71
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:22,940
All right?

72
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:25,700
However, if repair is not possible,

73
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:27,780
p53 induces apoptosis.

74
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:32,440
And I've already explained how apoptosis would occur.

75
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,400
Now, it's important to note that both copies of p53

76
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:40,240
must be knocked out for tumor formation.

77
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:42,000
And the way I think about this

78
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,680
is it's sort of like an intersection, right?

79
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:45,200
These are traffic cops,

80
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,740
and if one traffic cop goes to lunch,

81
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:51,460
then the other traffic cop can sort of man the intersection,

82
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:53,080
and there wouldn't really be any accidents.

83
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:56,660
But if both traffic cops are not at the intersection,

84
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:58,260
the intersection becomes chaotic,

85
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:00,080
and there's an increased risk for accidents.

86
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,880
And so the only way to develop tumors

87
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:05,280
is to knock out both copies of p53.

88
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:09,380
And that's called Knudsen's two-hit hypothesis.

89
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:10,420
All right?

90
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:13,240
That both copies of p53 must be knocked out

91
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:17,280
for tumor formation is this notion

92
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:18,780
that both copies must be knocked out

93
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:20,800
is called Knudsen's two-hit hypothesis.

94
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:25,300
Now, loss of p53 is seen in more than 50% of cancers.

95
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:30,940
Usually, both copies of p53 are knocked out somatically.

96
00:04:31,460 --> 00:04:33,740
However, it is possible that a patient could inherit

97
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:36,200
one bad copy of p53 from a parent,

98
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:38,300
and that's called the Lee-Fraumeni syndrome.

99
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:41,520
And the Lee-Fraumeni syndrome is associated

100
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,820
with an increased risk for multiple types

101
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:45,600
of carcinomas and sarcomas.

102
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:48,260
So that's a little bit about p53.

103
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:51,000
Another important tumor suppressor gene

104
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,220
is retinoblastoma.

105
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:55,580
And retinoblastoma also regulates progression

106
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:56,980
from G1 to S phase.

107
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:00,000
And the idea here is that,

108
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:01,840
I'll just do this as a picture,

109
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:05,740
when the cell wants to go from G1 to S phase,

110
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:08,540
one of the key molecules that it needs

111
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:11,660
in order to do that is called E2F.

112
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:15,040
So E2F is necessary to go from G1 to S phase,

113
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,320
and gets who holds on to E2F.

114
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:22,340
So E2F is normally bound by retinoblastoma.

115
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:25,300
And in order for E2F to be released,

116
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:27,580
retinoblastoma must be phosphorylated.

117
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:30,000
And when retinoblastoma is phosphorylated,

118
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:31,360
it becomes very happy,

119
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:33,340
and it releases E2F,

120
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:35,820
and the release of E2F then allows the cell

121
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:37,740
to go from G1 to S phase.

122
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:41,760
So that is the sort of the normal function

123
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:42,800
of retinoblastoma.

124
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:45,440
Now if there's a mutation in retinoblastoma,

125
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:47,300
then E2F is free,

126
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:50,220
and the free E2F will allow cells

127
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:53,740
to easily go from the G1 to the S phase,

128
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:56,820
and that will then result in the formation of a tumor.

129
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:59,980
So this is why loss of retinoblastoma

130
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:03,340
increases or results in the formation of tumor.

131
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:05,300
So let's go back and read.

132
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:11,680
So retinoblastoma regulates progression

133
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:13,460
from G1 to the S phase.

134
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:16,800
Retinoblastoma holds the E2F transcription factor,

135
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:19,480
which is necessary for a transition to the S phase.

136
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:21,820
Normally, E2F is released

137
00:06:21,820 --> 00:06:23,800
when retinoblastoma is phosphorylated.

138
00:06:23,980 --> 00:06:25,700
And who phosphorylates retinoblastoma?

139
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:29,000
Retinoblastoma, cyclin D, CDK4 complex,

140
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:31,300
which has the ability to phosphorylate proteins.

141
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:35,500
If retinoblastoma is mutated,

142
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:39,500
that results in constitutively free E2F.

143
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:41,600
So for E2F will be free,

144
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:47,460
and that allows for uncontrolled progression

145
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:48,580
through the cell cycle.

146
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:52,040
So that's the consequence of retinoblastoma mutation.

147
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:54,120
Now again, same idea.

148
00:06:54,120 --> 00:06:56,880
Both copies of retinoblastoma must be knocked out

149
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:57,880
for tumor formation,

150
00:06:58,420 --> 00:07:00,680
and that's called Knudsen's two-hit hypothesis.

151
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:06,040
If a patient has sporadic mutations

152
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:10,020
resulting in both copies of retinoblastoma being knocked out,

153
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:12,720
the patient will develop unilateral retinoblastoma.

154
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:17,980
However, if the patient has germline mutation of retinoblastoma,

155
00:07:18,360 --> 00:07:22,560
the patient is more likely to develop bilateral retinoblastoma,

156
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:25,460
and they also can develop osteosarcoma.

157
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:28,420
Now, I think this requires a little bit of explanation,

158
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:31,300
so I'm going to take a moment to back away

159
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:32,960
and explain a principle here.

160
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:35,040
Now let's take,

161
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:36,560
so what is retinoblastoma?

162
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:38,660
Retinoblastoma is a tumor

163
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:40,860
that develops in the back of the eye,

164
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:43,920
and I've included a picture here in your text,

165
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:44,940
and here is the eyeball,

166
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:46,100
and this is here,

167
00:07:46,220 --> 00:07:48,420
the tumor sort of in the back of the eye

168
00:07:48,420 --> 00:07:49,840
arising from the retina,

169
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:51,220
and that's a retinoblastoma.

170
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:53,420
Now let's take for a moment

171
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:56,420
a very simple example.

172
00:07:56,420 --> 00:07:58,140
I'm going to go to my whiteboard here

173
00:07:58,140 --> 00:07:59,600
and just explain something.

174
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:03,140
Let's pretend that there is a patient,

175
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:06,880
and we're going to draw a couple copies of retinoblastoma.

176
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:08,940
So this is retinoblastoma.

177
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:12,400
These are normal retinoblastoma genes

178
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:14,080
on their chromosomes,

179
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:15,800
and retinoblastoma,

180
00:08:15,960 --> 00:08:17,860
one copy is inherited from mom,

181
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:19,540
and one copy is inherited from dad,

182
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:21,340
and so this is a child,

183
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:24,740
and they've got two normal copies of retinoblastoma.

184
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:25,260
Okay?

185
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:26,640
Now they've got,

186
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:28,960
let's say in the back of the eye,

187
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:31,480
they've got billions of cells

188
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:32,900
sitting in the back of the eye,

189
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:35,280
and each cell has two normal copies

190
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:36,260
of retinoblastoma.

191
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:39,500
Now in order for a tumor to develop,

192
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,600
one copy of retinoblastoma

193
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:44,380
must be knocked out, right?

194
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:47,620
And then in the exact same cell,

195
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:49,840
that one cell in a billion,

196
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:53,000
the second copy must also be knocked out.

197
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:55,420
That's the only way,

198
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:57,000
by knocking out both copies,

199
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,520
that the tumor retinoblastoma

200
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:00,500
is going to form.

201
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:01,720
All right?

202
00:09:01,980 --> 00:09:03,600
So what's the probability

203
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:06,720
that one in a billion cells

204
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:08,340
would actually get hit,

205
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:11,160
the exact same cell would get hit twice,

206
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:13,080
and both copies would be knocked out?

207
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:14,160
It's essentially zero.

208
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:15,320
And that's why retinoblastoma

209
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:17,720
is essentially almost never seen.

210
00:09:17,780 --> 00:09:19,320
It's a very, very rare tumor,

211
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:20,620
and it's very unlikely

212
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:21,560
that you would ever see it

213
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:23,160
except on examinations.

214
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:27,300
And so that's why I say here in your text

215
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:29,380
that a sporadic mutation,

216
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:30,420
the most,

217
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:32,500
that a sporadic mutation,

218
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:34,880
the most those patients would ever develop

219
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:38,220
is a unilateral retinoblastoma.

220
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:41,360
Now let's take the other extreme,

221
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:43,100
and that is let's say that

222
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:44,660
the patient has,

223
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:45,480
I don't know,

224
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:46,660
retinoblastoma,

225
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:47,180
okay,

226
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:48,800
here and here.

227
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:50,980
These are the father and the mother.

228
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:52,160
And let's pretend

229
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:54,220
that one of the parents

230
00:09:54,220 --> 00:09:55,240
has a bad gene.

231
00:09:55,340 --> 00:09:56,300
We'll put an X through it.

232
00:09:56,680 --> 00:09:57,740
Now what's going to happen

233
00:09:57,740 --> 00:09:59,020
is that the child

234
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:00,200
is going to inherit

235
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:02,240
one bad gene

236
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:03,400
and one good gene,

237
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:03,940
correct?

238
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:05,820
Let's just make that as an example

239
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:06,900
that they happen to inherit

240
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:07,640
one bad gene

241
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:08,360
and one good gene.

242
00:10:08,780 --> 00:10:10,420
Now in the back of the eye,

243
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:12,420
every single cell,

244
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:14,600
all the one billion cells,

245
00:10:14,700 --> 00:10:15,560
just as an example,

246
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:17,120
in the back of the eye

247
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:19,560
are actually going to contain

248
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:21,560
or already begin with

249
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:22,500
one bad gene

250
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:23,300
because they inherited

251
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:24,040
one bad gene.

252
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:25,620
Now what's the probability

253
00:10:25,620 --> 00:10:27,220
that an additional mutation

254
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:28,120
could come in

255
00:10:28,120 --> 00:10:30,180
and hit the good gene?

256
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:30,500
Sorry,

257
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:31,560
this is a good gene here.

258
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:32,640
What's the probability

259
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:33,500
that that could happen?

260
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:34,260
It's pretty likely.

261
00:10:34,460 --> 00:10:34,700
It's not,

262
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:36,100
it wouldn't be unheard of.

263
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:37,420
And so these patients,

264
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:38,720
because the whole

265
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:39,860
back of the eye

266
00:10:39,860 --> 00:10:41,060
is sort of like a field

267
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:42,160
ready to be mutated,

268
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:43,640
if any one of these cells

269
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:44,180
gets hit,

270
00:10:44,620 --> 00:10:45,080
they're going to get

271
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:45,960
a retinoblastoma.

272
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:47,020
And similarly,

273
00:10:47,180 --> 00:10:47,940
if any one of these

274
00:10:47,940 --> 00:10:48,880
billions of cells

275
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:50,340
in the back of the other eye

276
00:10:50,340 --> 00:10:50,820
get hit,

277
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:51,540
they're going to get

278
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:52,660
another retinoblastoma.

279
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:54,140
And that's not too hard

280
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:54,680
to occur

281
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:56,880
because there's so many cells

282
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:58,100
that could easily

283
00:10:58,100 --> 00:10:59,300
get mutated.

284
00:10:59,780 --> 00:11:01,740
And so that's why

285
00:11:01,740 --> 00:11:03,560
patients with a germline mutation,

286
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:06,200
with a germline mutation,

287
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:07,240
have an increased risk

288
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:07,940
of usually get

289
00:11:07,940 --> 00:11:09,580
bilateral retinoblastoma.

290
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:11,520
And what's also important

291
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:12,380
for examinations,

292
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:13,480
there's an increased risk

293
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:15,020
of osteosarcoma.

294
00:11:15,120 --> 00:11:16,160
So that's something

295
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:17,660
to be familiar with as well.

296
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:19,140
Okay.

297
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:20,340
And the last,

298
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:21,120
or the third,

299
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:23,060
regulatory system

300
00:11:23,060 --> 00:11:23,660
are regulators

301
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:24,580
of apoptosis.

302
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:24,960
Now,

303
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:26,960
remember that apoptosis

304
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:28,080
needs to be regulated.

305
00:11:28,220 --> 00:11:28,940
And why does it need

306
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:29,560
to be regulated?

307
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:32,200
Because apoptosis,

308
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:34,520
you want apoptosis

309
00:11:34,520 --> 00:11:36,140
to not occur

310
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:37,080
in a normal cell.

311
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:37,640
You don't want

312
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:38,600
a normal cell to die.

313
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:39,580
So you want to prevent

314
00:11:39,580 --> 00:11:41,100
apoptosis in a normal cell.

315
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:42,420
But you want to promote

316
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:44,120
apoptosis in mutated cells

317
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:45,780
because you don't want

318
00:11:45,780 --> 00:11:47,280
cells whose DNA

319
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:49,140
cannot be repaired.

320
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:50,380
If there's DNA

321
00:11:50,380 --> 00:11:51,240
and it cannot be repaired,

322
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:52,640
you want apoptosis

323
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:53,300
to occur

324
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:54,640
in that mutated cell.

325
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:56,240
And so apoptosis

326
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:57,300
is highly regulated.

327
00:11:57,760 --> 00:11:58,720
One of the important

328
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:00,120
regulators of apoptosis

329
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:01,200
is BCL2.

330
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:02,520
Now,

331
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:03,640
what does BCL2 do?

332
00:12:03,740 --> 00:12:04,400
I've told you this

333
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:05,800
time and time again,

334
00:12:05,940 --> 00:12:06,960
but we'll repeat it

335
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:07,720
one more time.

336
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:09,500
BCL2 stabilizes

337
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:10,740
the mitochondrial membrane

338
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:12,140
so that cytochrome C

339
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:13,120
cannot be released.

340
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:15,260
If BCL2 gets disrupted,

341
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:16,340
cytochrome C

342
00:12:16,340 --> 00:12:17,120
can leak out,

343
00:12:17,380 --> 00:12:18,400
which then activates

344
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:19,280
caspases

345
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:20,800
and subsequently results

346
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:21,960
in apoptosis.

347
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:23,560
So this is BCL2.

348
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:24,460
Now,

349
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:25,840
one of the high-yield

350
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:27,000
principles to be familiar

351
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:28,440
with regarding BCL2

352
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:29,900
is that BCL2

353
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:31,060
is overexpressed

354
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:32,400
in follicular lymphoma.

355
00:12:33,020 --> 00:12:33,320
Okay,

356
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:34,240
let's go back.

357
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:34,920
Now,

358
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:35,600
basically,

359
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:36,780
the idea here

360
00:12:36,780 --> 00:12:38,640
is that in follicular lymphoma

361
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:39,380
you get something

362
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:41,560
